nid not in present:\n            continue\n        # Check if removable and not required symbol and no retained children\n        if not n.get('removable', False):\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check for retained children (any child still present)\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\n        has_child = any(cid in present for cid in children)\n        if has_child:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove: check if required symbols still present after removal\n        # We need to check if for each required symbol, there is a node in present\\{nid} with that symbol\n        # Since n is not a required symbol itself, removing it does not affect required symbols except if it was the only one? But we already ensured symbol not required, so no.\n        # However, there might be required symbols that appear only in this node's descendants? But descendants are not present because children are not present (since no retained children). However, what about deeper descendants that were already removed? They are not present. So no issue.\n        # Actually if a required symbol appears only in a subtree that has been completely removed, that's a problem but that subtree removal would have been prevented earlier. So safe.\n        # We'll still check: for each sym in required_symbols, check if there exists a node in present - {nid} with that symbol.\n        # Since nid's symbol is not required, it's already satisfied.\n        # But to be safe, we do the check.\n        required_ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            found = False\n            for pid in present:\n                if pid != nid and node_by_id[pid]['symbol'] == sym:\n                    found = True\n                    break\n            if not found:\n                required_ok = False\n                break\n        if required_ok:\n            present.remove(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(present)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nBut note: The check for retained children is sufficient to ensure that the node is a leaf in the current present set. However, there could be a case where a node has children that are not present because they were previously removed, but those children themselves might have contained required symbols that are now lost. However, since we only removed leaves after checking required symbols, that should be fine.\n\nTesting with given fixtures: The first case has nodes: root (depth0, removable false), n1(s1, depth1, removable false), n2(s2, depth1, removable true), n3(s0, depth1, removable false), n4(s1, depth2, removable true), n5(s2, depth2, removable false), n6(s0, depth2, removable true). required_symbols: root, s1. So nodes with symbol s1: n1 (not removable) and n4 (removable). n4 is removable but its symbol is required? s1 is required, so if we treat symbols in required_symbols as non-removable, then n4 cannot be removed. But the algorithm might remove n4? The rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That would prevent removal of n4 because its symbol s1 is required. However, the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would allow removal if another s1 exists (n1). The more restrictive interpretation (never remove a node with required symbol) would make n4 non-removable. Which is correct? Looking at typical Perses, you can remove a node even if its symbol is required, as long as the required symbol still appears elsewhere. But the instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" is ambiguous: it could mean a node that is required (as in the root) or a node whose symbol is required. I think it means a node that is itself required, i.e., the root and possibly other nodes marked as required? But the data h